public with sharing class CasePriorityHandler {
    public static void setPriority(List<Case> newCases) {
        if (newCases == null || newCases.isEmpty()) {
            return;
        }

        // Bulkify: collect all AccountIds once, then query Accounts once.
        Set<Id> accountIds = new Set<Id>();
        for (Case c : newCases) {
            if (c.AccountId != null) {
                accountIds.add(c.AccountId);
            }
        }

        Map<Id, Account> hotAccountsById = new Map<Id, Account>();
        if (!accountIds.isEmpty()) {
            for (Account a : [
                SELECT Id, Rating
                FROM Account
                WHERE Id IN :accountIds
                  AND Rating = 'Hot'
            ]) {
                hotAccountsById.put(a.Id, a);
            }
        }

        for (Case c : newCases) {
            if (c.AccountId != null && hotAccountsById.containsKey(c.AccountId)) {
                c.Priority = 'High';
            }
        }
    }
}
```

I did not put the SOQL query inside the case loop. With the ERP integration creating up to 200 cases in one transaction, a query inside the loop would issue up to 200 SOQL queries and exceed the 100-query governor limit. The handler instead collects the distinct `AccountId` values first and runs one SOQL query for the whole trigger batch, then uses a `Map` to set `Priority`. The behavior is the same: Hot accounts get `High`, and every other case, including cases without an account, keeps its original `Priority`.

I also declared the handler `with sharing`. If your Email-to-Case or integration user cannot read the Accounts, either grant read access or change the class to `without sharing`.

**Deviation (and why):** the callout cannot be made *inside* the trigger body. An `after insert` trigger still has uncommitted DML in the transaction, so any HTTP request from it fails immediately with *"You have uncommitted work pending. Please commit or rollback before calling out."* The trigger therefore hands the new Accounts to `ErpCustomerSync`, a Queueable that performs the callout asynchronously — still **one HTTP request per Account**, so the ERP team can trace every Account individually, and the job only runs if the inserting transaction commits.

Bulk-safety notes baked into the design: the trigger enqueues exactly **one** job (platform limit is 50 `enqueueJob` calls per transaction), the job makes at most 50 callouts per transaction (platform limit is 100) and **chains exactly one** follow-up job for the remainder (only one chained job may be added per executing job). A 200-Account marketplace batch therefore becomes 4 jobs × 50 single-customer requests.

