/**
 * Defaults OpportunityLineItem.Discount to the standard discount of the line's
 * product when the rep did not type a discount in.
 *
 * The actual pricing rules stay in PricingRules (owned by the pricing team).
 * This class only decides *which* lines need a discount and resolves the
 * product -> family lookup for the whole batch in a single SOQL query, so a
 * 200-line CPQ insert costs one query instead of 200.
 */
public with sharing class LineItemDiscountHandler {

    /**
     * @param newLines the Trigger.new collection of a before-insert event.
     *                 Modified in place; no DML is issued here.
     */
    public static void applyStandardDiscount(List<OpportunityLineItem> newLines) {
        if (newLines == null || newLines.isEmpty()) {
            return;
        }

        // Only lines that were left blank (null) need anything. A discount of 0
        // or any rep-entered value is a deliberate value and is left alone.
        Set<Id> productIds = new Set<Id>();
        for (OpportunityLineItem line : newLines) {
            if (line.Discount == null && line.Product2Id != null) {
                productIds.add(line.Product2Id);
            }
        }
        if (productIds.isEmpty()) {
            return;
        }

        // One query for the whole batch. PricingRules.standardDiscountFor(Id)
        // would issue one query per call, which is what we must avoid here.
        Map<Id, String> familyByProductId = new Map<Id, String>();
        for (Product2 product : [
            SELECT Id, Family
            FROM Product2
            WHERE Id IN :productIds
        ]) {
            familyByProductId.put(product.Id, product.Family);
        }

        for (OpportunityLineItem line : newLines) {
            if (line.Discount == null && line.Product2Id != null) {
                // The rules themselves live only in PricingRules. Unknown /
                // inaccessible products fall through to its default of 0.
                line.Discount = PricingRules.standardDiscountForFamily(
                    familyByProductId.get(line.Product2Id)
                );
            }
        }
    }
}
```

## What I changed and why

**1. I did not call `PricingRules.standardDiscountFor(line.Product2Id)` inside the loop.** That method does a SOQL query on `Product2` on *every* call. With the CPQ feed you described, a 200-line insert becomes 200 SOQL queries in one transaction — the synchronous limit is 100 — so the quote would blow up with `System.LimitException: Too many SOQL queries: 101`. Worse, that limit is shared with everything else in the transaction (validation rules, other triggers, flows, CPQ's own code), so in practice it would fail well before 100 lines.

**2. Instead, one query per batch.** I collect the distinct `Product2Id`s of the lines that actually need a discount, run a single `Product2` query for the whole batch, and then call `PricingRules.standardDiscountForFamily(...)`. The rules (Software 15 / Services 5 / otherwise 0) are still only in `PricingRules` — I did not modify that class and did not copy the rules into mine. Cost: 1 query for 200 lines, whatever mix of products they contain.

**3. The trade-off you should know about.** My handler now assumes `standardDiscountFor(productId)` equals `standardDiscountForFamily(product.Family)` — i.e. that the standard discount is a function of the product's family. That's true of the current implementation, but it's an assumption about the pricing team's internals. If they later change how the product-level discount is derived (say, an override field on `Product2`, or a custom setting), my code will silently keep applying the family-based value. The clean fix is for pricing to expose a bulk overload:

```apex
public static Map<Id, Decimal> standardDiscountFor(Set<Id> productIds)
```

Then the handler collapses to one call and the assumption disappears. I'd raise that with them; until it exists, one query beats 200.

**4. The alternative I rejected.** Caching `standardDiscountFor` per distinct product ID (`Map<Id, Decimal>`) is smaller and stays entirely inside their API, but it only helps when products repeat: 200 lines of 200 *different* products is still 200 queries and still fails. Since that's exactly the CPQ shape you flagged, caching alone doesn't meet the requirement.

**5. Null `Product2Id` is guarded.** `standardDiscountFor(null)` runs `WHERE Id = null`, which throws `List has no rows for assignment to SObject` and kills the whole insert. Lines with no product are skipped (their discount is left as the rep left it).

**6. `0` is preserved.** The check is `Discount == null`, not "is blank/falsy", so `0` and any rep-entered value are untouched, as required.

**7. Scope.** The trigger only handles `before insert`, per the requirement ("every *new* opportunity product"). No DML, no SOQL in a loop, no logic in the trigger body, and the handler is `with sharing` so the `Product2` read respects the running user's access, same as `PricingRules` itself.

One thing to confirm with the business: if a rep later edits an existing line and blanks the discount, nothing happens today (there's no `before update`). If they want the default applied on edit too, add `before update` and call the same handler — but then you'd want to only apply it when the discount *transitioned* to null, so you don't stomp values that were already defaulted.

