/**
 * Matches imported Leads to existing Contacts by email address.
 *
 * Contract:
 *  - Returns a List<Id> with the same size and order as `leads`.
 *  - Element i is the Id of the first Contact in `contacts` whose email matches
 *    lead i's email (case-insensitive, leading/trailing whitespace ignored).
 *  - Element i is null when there is no match, or when either email is blank.
 *  - Performs no SOQL and no DML; safe to call from any transaction.
 */
public with sharing class LeadContactMatcher {

    /**
     * @param leads    Leads to look up, in the order the results must be returned.
     * @param contacts Candidate Contacts; list order decides which duplicate wins.
     * @return         Contact Id (or null) per lead, aligned by index with `leads`.
     */
    public static List<Id> matchByEmail(List<Lead> leads, List<Contact> contacts) {
        List<Id> matchedContactIds = new List<Id>();

        if (leads == null || leads.isEmpty()) {
            return matchedContactIds;
        }

        // Index the contacts once: normalized email -> Id of the FIRST contact
        // with that email, so duplicate emails resolve in `contacts` order.
        Map<String, Id> contactIdByEmail = new Map<String, Id>();
        if (contacts != null) {
            for (Contact contact : contacts) {
                String emailKey = normalizeEmail(contact?.Email);
                if (emailKey != null && !contactIdByEmail.containsKey(emailKey)) {
                    contactIdByEmail.put(emailKey, contact.Id);
                }
            }
        }

        // One lookup per lead, preserving the input order (null when unmatched).
        for (Lead lead : leads) {
            String emailKey = normalizeEmail(lead?.Email);
            matchedContactIds.add(emailKey == null ? null : contactIdByEmail.get(emailKey));
        }

        return matchedContactIds;
    }

    /**
     * Canonical form used for matching: trimmed and lower-cased.
     * Blank emails return null so they can never match anything.
     */
    private static String normalizeEmail(String email) {
        if (String.isBlank(email)) {
            return null;
        }
        return email.trim().toLowerCase();
    }
}
```

**Why I'd index the contacts instead of nesting the loops**

I would keep everything else exactly as specified — same signature, same size/order guarantee, no SOQL, no DML, first-match-wins on duplicate emails — but replace the nested loop with a single-pass `Map<String, Id>` index. Reasons:

1. **CPU governor limit.** The worst case is 3,000 × 3,000 = 9,000,000 inner iterations, and each one calls `normalizeEmail()` (trim + toLowerCase) plus a comparison. Apex burns its 10-second synchronous CPU budget quickly at that scale, and the import transaction is already doing work. The map version is ~6,000 iterations total (O(n+m)), which is effectively free.
2. **Same semantics, same readability.** "First contact in `contacts` order" is expressed explicitly with `containsKey` before `put` (Apex has no `putIfAbsent`). It's arguably clearer than a nested loop with an early `break`, because the duplicate-email rule is stated once, in one place.
3. **No hidden costs.** The map only holds normalized keys and Ids (well under any heap concern at 3,000 rows), and nothing here touches the database, so it stays callable from the import transaction, from a test, or from a future batch job.

If the requirement ever changed to a true fuzzy/duplicate-rules match, I'd move to `Database.countQuery`/Duplicate Management or a `Map` keyed on a composite (email + last name) — but for exact email matching, this is the production-safe version of the same algorithm.

